# Anserini Regressions: MS MARCO Passage Ranking

**Model**: SlideOn (using ONNX for on-the-fly query encoding)

This page describes regression experiments, integrated into Anserini's regression testing framework, using the [SlideOn](https://huggingface.co/lightonai/SlideOn) model on the [MS MARCO passage ranking task](https://github.com/microsoft/MSMARCO-Passage-Ranking).

In these experiments, we are using ONNX to perform query encoding on the fly.
See [SlideOn ONNX inference](../../onnx-slideon.md) for model requirements and query settings.

The exact configurations for these regressions are stored in [this YAML file](${yaml}).
Note that this page is automatically generated from [this template](${template}) as part of Anserini's regression pipeline, so do not modify this page directly; modify the template instead and then run `bin/build.sh` to rebuild the documentation.

First convert the pre-encoded Parquet vectors to JSONL as described below.
From one of our Waterloo servers (e.g., `orca`), the following command will perform the complete regression, end to end:

```bash
bin/run.sh io.anserini.reproduce.ReproduceFromDocumentCollection --index --verify --search --config ${test_name}
```

## Preparing the Collection

On our Waterloo servers, the pre-encoded Parquet files are located at `/store/collections/msmarco/msmarco-passage-slideon`.
These files contain `id`, `indices`, and FP16 `weights`, rather than the JSONL records required by `JsonVectorCollection`.
Convert them using [to_anserini.py](../../../src/main/python/slideon/to_anserini.py), copied from the [upstream script](https://huggingface.co/datasets/lightonai/SlideOn-sparse-vectors/blob/main/to_anserini.py):

```bash
python -m pip install 'pyarrow>=21' numpy
python src/main/python/slideon/to_anserini.py \
  /store/collections/msmarco/msmarco-passage-slideon \
  collections/msmarco/msmarco-passage-slideon-jsonl/corpus.jsonl.gz \
  --k 512 --scale 100
```

## Indexing

Sample indexing command:

```bash
${index_cmds}
```

The path `/path/to/${corpus}/` should point to the corpus converted above.

The important indexing options to note here are `-impact -pretokenized`: the first tells Anserini not to encode BM25 doc lengths into Lucene's norms (which is the default) and the second option says not to apply any additional tokenization on the pre-encoded tokens.
Upon completion, we should have an index with 8,841,823 documents.

For additional details, see explanation of [common indexing options](../../common-indexing-options.md).

## Retrieval

Topics and qrels are stored in a [centralized repo containing evaluation data](https://github.com/castorini/eval).
The regression experiments here evaluate on the 6980 dev set questions; see [this page](${root_path}/docs/experiments-msmarco-passage.md) for more details.

Use the default SlideOn query settings (q64, scale 100); overriding these settings changes the experiment.
The ONNX model is downloaded automatically on first use.

After indexing has completed, you should be able to perform retrieval as follows:

```bash
${ranking_cmds}
```

Evaluation can be performed using `trec_eval`:

```bash
${eval_cmds}
```

